  "activity": {
    "id": "act_retrograde_motion",
    "name": "The Puzzle of Retrograde Motion",
    "description": "A five-step AR investigation of Mars's apparent retrograde motion. Students first observe Mars looping across the constellations from Earth's surface, then examine the awkward geocentric epicycle explanation, switch to the outside heliocentric view to see Earth overtake Mars, connect this to the overtaking-car analogy, and finally compare the smaller loops of Jupiter and Saturn. Aligns with objectives on describing, explaining, contrasting, predicting, and comparing retrograde motion.",
    "workplace": "wp_retrograde_motion",
    "start": "step_observe_sky",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_observe_sky",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. Watch Mars Loop in the Night Sky",
          "description": "Point your device at the Night Sky Dome marker. Over the simulated weeks, watch Mars drift slowly eastward against the background stars, slow to a stop, reverse into a westward loop for about two months, and then resume its eastward march. Note the shape of the path Mars traces. Tap Continue when you have observed a full loop."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_skydome",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_starfield_image",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_retrograde_loop_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_mars",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Mars: eastward drift \u2192 westward loop \u2192 eastward again"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_skydome",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_intro",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_skydome",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_observe_sky"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_geocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. The Geocentric Struggle: Epicycles",
          "description": "In the ancient geocentric model, Earth sits still at the center and everything orbits it. To reproduce Mars's backward loop, astronomers had to add an epicycle \u2014 a small circle riding on Mars's main circle. Watch how contrived this machinery must be just to make the loop appear. Tap Continue to seek a simpler explanation."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_skydome",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_geocentric_epicycle_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_skydome",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_planets",
              "poi": "poi_zenith",
              "text": "Geocentric model: Mars needs an epicycle to loop"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_skydome",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_geocentric"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_heliocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. Step Outside: The Heliocentric View",
          "description": "Point your device at the Orbit Board marker to view the solar system from outside. Watch Earth and Mars each travel a smooth circular orbit around the Sun \u2014 no epicycles needed. Earth, on the inner orbit, moves faster than Mars. Detect the Earth marker to highlight our planet's path before continuing."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_heliocentric_orbits_anim",
              "poi": "poi_sun_center"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_earth",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_earth",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Earth \u2014 faster inner orbit"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_mars",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Mars \u2014 slower outer orbit"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_board",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_earth",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "thing_earth"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_heliocentric"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_overtake",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "4. Why the Loop Appears: Earth Overtakes Mars",
          "description": "As Earth catches up to and passes the slower-moving Mars, our shifting line of sight makes Mars appear to slide backward against the stars \u2014 exactly like a slower car in the next lane seeming to drift backward as you pass it. The loop is an illusion of relative motion, not a real reversal. Tap Continue to compare other planets."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_overtake_diagram_image",
              "poi": "poi_side_view"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_overtake",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_planets",
              "poi": "poi_side_view",
              "text": "Retrograde = Earth overtaking Mars (relative motion)"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_board",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_overtake"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_compare_giants",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "5. Compare Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn",
          "description": "Point your device at the Giants marker to view all three loops side by side. Because Jupiter and Saturn orbit much farther out, the relative-motion effect between them and Earth is smaller, so their retrograde loops are tighter and less dramatic than Mars's. Use this to predict which distant planets loop the least. This completes the lab."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_giants",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_giants_loops_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_giants",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_planets",
              "poi": "poi_jupiter",
              "text": "Jupiter \u2014 smaller loop"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_giants",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_planets",
              "poi": "poi_saturn",
              "text": "Saturn \u2014 smallest loop (farthest)"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_retrograde_loop_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_mars",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Mars \u2014 largest loop (closest)"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_giants",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "thing_giants"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_compare_giants"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}